    {
      "dom_id": "question-346185",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "Given that ${\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=5$ and ${\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=-1,$ \nthen what is ${\\int }_{0}^{6}(4-5f(x))\\,\\text{d}x?$\n\n${\\int }_{0}^{6}(4-5f(x))\\,\\text{d}x=$ \n{{field-1}}",
      "worked_solution": "From the adjacent intervals rule, we know that\n\n$$\n{\\int }_{0}^{2}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,+\\,{\\int }_{2}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,=\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x.\n$$\n\nPlugging in the known information gives\n\n$$\n5+(-1)\\,=\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x\\,⇒\\,{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x=4.\n$$\n\nFinally, compute the desired integral:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\int }_{0}^{6}(4-5f(x))\\,\\text{d}x & =4{\\int }_{0}^{6}1\\,\\text{d}x\\,-\\,5{\\int }_{0}^{6}f(x)\\,\\text{d}x \\\\  & =4\\cdot x{|}_{0}^{6}\\,-\\,5(4) \\\\  & =4(6-0)-20 \\\\  & =24-20 \\\\  & =4\\end{aligned}\n$$",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [
        {
          "key": "field-1",
          "type": "blank",
          "dom_id": "freeResponseTextbox-1",
