    {
      "dom_id": "question-11153",
      "name": null,
      "problem": "What is the limit of the sequence ${a}_{n}=\\frac{8{n}^{5}-2n}{8{n}^{4}-2}$ for $n\\ge 1?$",
      "worked_solution": "The dominant term in the denominator is $8{n}^{4},$ so we divide the numerator and the denominator by ${n}^{4}$ and get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{8{n}^{5}-2n}{8{n}^{4}-2} & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(\\frac{8{n}^{5}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{2n}{{n}^{4}})}{(\\frac{8{n}^{4}}{{n}^{4}}-\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{(8n-\\frac{2}{{n}^{3}})}{(8-\\frac{2}{{n}^{4}})} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }\\frac{8n-0}{8-0} \\\\  & ={lim}_{n\\to \\infty }n \\\\  & =\\infty .\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe limit does not exist, so the sequence diverges.",
      "calculator_instructions": "",
      "fields": [],
      "answer_fields_complete": null,
      "result": "Correct",
      "proof_feedback": null,
